# Lab 07 - Linux Networking Basics

## Objective

Identify this machine's network interfaces, IP configuration, gateway, and listening ports.

---

## Environment

- Operating System: Kali Linux
- User: hassan

---

## Commands Executed

```bash
ip addr
ip route
ss -tuln
```

---

## Findings

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Hostname | kali |
| Interface | eth0 |
| IP Address | 192.168.18.181/24 |
| MAC Address | 00:0c:29:1b:9f:22 |
| Network | 192.168.18.0/24 |
| Broadcast | 192.168.18.255 |
| Default Gateway | 192.168.18.1 |
| Loopback | 127.0.0.1 |

`ss -tuln` output showed one TCP listening socket on port 22:

```text
tcp   LISTEN   0   128   0.0.0.0:22   0.0.0.0:*
```

This indicates SSH may be the service listening on this port.

---

## Skills Gained

- Reading `ip addr` output: interfaces, IP address, CIDR, MAC address
- Understanding the difference between IP address and MAC address
- Identifying the default gateway with `ip route`
- Interpreting `ss -tuln` output to find listening ports
- Connecting an open port to a potential service and attack surface

---

## Conclusion

This lab established a baseline understanding of the machine's own network identity - interface, IP, gateway, and listening ports. This baseline is the starting point for any future network reconnaissance or troubleshooting work.
